.\" Manual page for git-clone-for-worktrees.
.\" Hand-written roff. There is no generator; edit this file directly.
.TH GIT\-CLONE\-FOR\-WORKTREES 1 "2026-08-18" "git-refresh" "Git Extras"
.
.SH NAME
git\-clone\-for\-worktrees \- clone a repository as a bare repo with its
default branch checked out beside it
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git clone\-for\-worktrees
.I repo
.RB [ \-\-upstream
.IR repo ]
.br
.B git clone\-for\-worktrees
.B \-h
.PP
Also installed as
.BR git\-clone\-for\-worktrees ,
and reachable as
.B git clone\-for\-worktrees
without an alias: git looks for a
.B git\-clone\-for\-worktrees
in
.B PATH
before it consults aliases.
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git clone\-for\-worktrees
clones
.I repo
into
.IB ./ name /.git
as a bare repository and checks its default branch out to
.IB ./ name / <branch>\fR,
ready for
.BR git\-new\-worktree (1)
to add the rest:
.PP
.EX
myrepo/
  .git/     the bare repository: history, objects, config
  main/     a worktree for the default branch
.EE
.PP
.I name
is the last path component of
.IR repo ,
with any
.B .git
suffix removed. The directory must not already exist.
.PP
Only the default branch is checked out. Every other branch is a worktree you add
when you want it, rather than one directory per branch the remote happens to
have.
.PP
The run does the four things a bare clone does not do by itself:
.IP \(bu 3
Sets
.B remote.origin.fetch
to
.BR +refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/* .
A bare clone leaves it unset, so
.B refs/remotes/origin/*
stays empty and every later fetch brings back nothing. The worktrees need those
refs to track against.
.IP \(bu 3
Adds and fetches
.B upstream
when the repository is a fork; see
.B Forks
below.
.IP \(bu 3
Resolves origin's default branch with
.B "git remote set\-head origin \-\-auto"
and adds a worktree for it. If origin has no branches there is nothing to check
out and the run fails.
.IP \(bu 3
Sets that worktree's upstream to
.IR origin/ <default>.
A bare clone copies branches into
.B refs/heads
with no upstream, so the checkout would otherwise sit disconnected from the
origin branch it was made from.
.PP
Nothing here acts on a repository you happen to be standing in:
.BR GIT_DIR ,
.BR GIT_WORK_TREE ,
.B GIT_INDEX_FILE
and
.B GIT_PREFIX
are unset before anything else runs.
.
.SS Repository spec
.I repo
is either
.IR owner/name ,
which becomes an ssh URL
.RI ( git@github.com: owner / name .git ),
or any URL git already understands. A scheme
.RB ( ://\& ),
an scp\-style host
.RI ( user@host : path ),
or a filesystem path
.RB ( / ,
.BR ./ ,
.BR ../ ,
.BR ~/ )
is passed through untouched. Anything else is refused rather than guessed at.
.
.SS Forks
When
.I repo
is given as
.IR owner/name ,
.BR gh (1)
is installed, and the repository is a GitHub fork, its parent is added as the
.B upstream
remote automatically and fetched. That is the one thing git cannot work out from
the URL.
.B \-\-upstream
names one by hand, and overrides the automatic lookup.
.PP
.BR gh (1)
is optional. Without it,
.B \-\-upstream
is the only way to get an
.B upstream
remote, and nothing else changes.
.PP
Once
.B upstream
exists, every
.BR git\-refresh (1)
run begins by fast\-forwarding origin's default branch from it, after which
everything is a plain origin workflow.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I repo
The repository to clone.
.I owner/name
or any URL git understands. Exactly one is expected.
.TP
.BI \-\-upstream " repo"
Add this as the
.B upstream
remote and fetch it. Takes the same forms as
.IR repo .
Only needed to override the automatic fork lookup, or when
.BR gh (1)
is not installed.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit 0.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR GIT_DIR ", " GIT_WORK_TREE ", " GIT_INDEX_FILE ", " GIT_PREFIX
Unset before anything else runs, so the clone cannot be aimed at a repository
you are standing in.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB ./ name /.git
The bare repository created by the clone.
.TP
.IB ./ name / <default\-branch>
The worktree for origin's default branch, tracking
.IR origin/ <default\-branch>.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The bare repository and the default branch's worktree were created.
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error: the target directory exists, git is missing, the repository spec
could not be understood,
.B \-\-upstream
was given without a value, more than one repository was named, origin has no
default branch, or git itself failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option, or no repository given.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Clone over ssh into
.IR ./git\-refresh/ :
.PP
.EX
git clone\-for\-worktrees gregswift/git\-refresh
cd git\-refresh/main
.EE
.PP
Clone a fork and name its parent yourself:
.PP
.EX
git clone\-for\-worktrees gregswift/fork \-\-upstream upstream\-owner/repo
.EE
.PP
Clone from a URL git already understands:
.PP
.EX
git clone\-for\-worktrees https://github.com/gregswift/git\-refresh.git
.EE
.PP
Then add branches and keep them current:
.PP
.EX
gwt my\-feature
git refresh \-\-all
.EE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-refresh (1),
.BR git\-new\-worktree (1),
.BR git\-worktree (1),
.BR git\-clone (1),
.BR git\-rebase (1),
.BR git\-push (1),
.BR gh (1)
.
